"""
PANDAS CHEAT SHEET: .query() vs Boolean Indexing + .size()

==================================================
1. FILTERING ROWS
==================================================

🔹 BOOLEAN INDEXING (most fundamental)

df[df['col'] > 5]
df[(df['A'] > 0) & (df['B'] < 10)]
df[df['col'].isin([1,2,3])]
df[~df['col'].isna()]

✔ Pros:
- Works everywhere
- Full Python flexibility
- Handles complex logic

❌ Cons:
- More verbose
- Harder to read with many conditions


--------------------------------------------------

🔹 .query() (cleaner, SQL-like)

df.query("col > 5")
df.query("A > 0 and B < 10")
df.query("col in [1,2,3]")
df.query("col.notna()")

✔ Pros:
- Cleaner, more readable
- Great for chaining
- Looks like SQL

❌ Cons:
- Column names must be valid variables
- Limited Python support inside string


--------------------------------------------------

🔹 USING VARIABLES IN .query()

threshold = 10

df.query("col > @threshold")

# @ pulls in Python variable


==================================================
2. WHEN TO USE WHICH
==================================================

USE .query() WHEN:
✔ Simple filtering
✔ You want readable code
✔ You're chaining operations

USE BOOLEAN INDEXING WHEN:
✔ Complex logic
✔ Using functions
✔ Column names have spaces/special chars


==================================================
3. GROUP COUNTING
==================================================

🔹 .size() → counts ROWS

df.groupby('A').size()

# counts how many rows per group


--------------------------------------------------

🔹 .count() → counts NON-NULL values

df.groupby('A').count()

# counts per column, ignores NaNs


--------------------------------------------------

🔹 BEST PRACTICE (most common pattern)

df.groupby(['A','B']).size().reset_index(name='count')


==================================================
4. COMMON PATTERNS (IMPORTANT)
==================================================

🔹 Filter + group + count

(
    df
    .query("A > 0")
    .groupby('B')
    .size()
    .reset_index(name='count')
)


--------------------------------------------------

🔹 Self-join + count (co-occurrence)

(
    df.merge(df, on='key')
      .query("id_x != id_y")
      .groupby(['id_x','id_y'])
      .size()
      .reset_index(name='count')
)


==================================================
5. QUICK MEMORY RULES
==================================================

.query()  → "filter like SQL"
[]        → "filter like Python"
.size()   → "count rows"
.count()  → "count non-null values"

.reset_index(name='count') → clean output


==================================================
6. MINI EXAMPLES
==================================================

# Boolean indexing
df[df['score'] > 80]

# Query equivalent
df.query("score > 80")

# Group count
df.groupby('team').size()

# Clean DataFrame
df.groupby('team').size().reset_index(name='n')
